/// `interop-boundary` is the boundary with Rust: a gandr definition called from Rust, and a Rust function called from gandr.
///
/// Implemented: a Rust caller holding a composed program calls a definition by name on values it builds and reads the answer back into its own arena; the call applies the arguments in order and leaves an answered function unforced, and a refused definition never reaches a run. An `extern "rust"` block of fixed-width members declares an interface that its source's definitions perform and the Rust caller's host answers.
/// Not implemented: no value crosses by Rust's default layout yet, neither `Option<&u32>` by its niche nor a list in two layouts that agree after transport; the checker admits no `extern` block against its function row where the block stands, and a source that imports a block does not see its interface (#2901).
///
/// The Rust caller is `crates/surface-dispatcher/tests/boundary.rs`; each call below is one it makes.
///
/// - see: corpus/model/interop/boundary/round-trip.gandr

// `double` and `less`.
import "../../model/interop/boundary/round-trip.gandr";

/// `double` at 21, the call the Rust caller makes first.
@[ runs("42") ]
def answer() -> Integer { double(21) }

/// `less` at 7 and 19: the order the Rust caller passes decides the sign.
@[ runs("-12") ]
def reversed() -> Integer { less(7, 19) }
